WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.680
In this lesson, we're looking at what happens when your source documents disagree.

2
00:00:04.239 --> 00:00:09.039
A well-designed synthesis pipeline doesn't just pick one source and move on.

3
00:00:09.039 --> 00:00:14.079
It detects the conflict, annotates it, and applies an explicit resolution strategy.

4
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.639
We'll cover detection, annotation patterns, resolution rules,

5
00:00:18.639 --> 00:00:22.719
and logging disagreements even after they're resolved. Let's get into it.

6
00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:26.000
Your sources disagree. What does the output say?

7
00:00:26.559 --> 00:00:31.920
Conflict annotation is the practice of surfacing that disagreement rather than hiding it.

8
00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:38.080
When sources contradict each other, an annotated synthesis tells the reader a conflict exists

9
00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:41.119
instead of pretending only one source ever spoke.

10
00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:46.479
A resolution strategy is the explicit rule you use to decide between them.

11
00:00:46.479 --> 00:00:51.200
Prefer newer, prefer authoritative, and escalate to human are the main options,

12
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.279
and we'll walk through each one.

13
00:00:53.279 --> 00:00:57.759
The averaging anti-pattern is the failure mode all of this guards against.

14
00:00:57.759 --> 00:01:01.759
It's what happens when a system silently blends conflicting sources

15
00:01:01.759 --> 00:01:03.759
or picks one without logging the choice.

16
00:01:04.319 --> 00:01:08.879
The reader gets a confident-sounding claim with no indication that another source

17
00:01:08.879 --> 00:01:09.839
said something different.

18
00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:15.680
The averaging anti-pattern is subtle because the output still looks good.

19
00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:18.800
The prose is fluent, the claim sounds certain,

20
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:23.040
and there's nothing in the text to suggest that another source disagreed.

21
00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:24.959
Here's what actually happened.

22
00:01:24.959 --> 00:01:28.239
The model encountered two incompatible claims.

23
00:01:28.239 --> 00:01:32.800
Instead of flagging the conflict, it either averaged them into a vague middle claim

24
00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:37.519
that neither source actually supports, or it chose one, discarded the other,

25
00:01:37.519 --> 00:01:40.319
and presented that choice as settled fact.

26
00:01:40.319 --> 00:01:43.440
The reader has no way to know a choice was made.

27
00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.639
They can't evaluate whether the right source was selected,

28
00:01:46.639 --> 00:01:51.519
and the discarded source may have been more accurate, more recent, or more authoritative.

29
00:01:52.239 --> 00:01:56.480
Downstream decisions get made on a foundation of false certainty.

30
00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:00.480
This is why conflict annotation isn't optional in high-stakes synthesis.

31
00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:04.800
The cost of hidden disagreement is higher than the cost of surfacing it.

32
00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:10.320
Conflict detection is the upstream step that makes annotation possible.

33
00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:15.600
Before your pipeline synthesizes anything, it needs a mechanism to identify

34
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:19.360
when two sources make incompatible claims about the same fact.

35
00:02:19.919 --> 00:02:22.960
The practical approach combines two techniques.

36
00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:25.119
First, semantic similarity.

37
00:02:25.119 --> 00:02:29.279
You find passages from different sources that are discussing the same topic.

38
00:02:29.279 --> 00:02:32.160
High similarity means they're making claims about the same thing.

39
00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:35.119
Second, entailment checking.

40
00:02:35.119 --> 00:02:40.800
You test whether one passage contradicts the other, supports it, or is simply unrelated.

41
00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:43.839
When two passages are semantically similar,

42
00:02:43.839 --> 00:02:48.880
but entailment scoring says they contradict each other, you have a detected conflict.

43
00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:53.759
The key insight is that detection should happen before synthesis, not after.

44
00:02:53.759 --> 00:02:56.320
If you wait until the output is already written,

45
00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:58.479
you're auditing a problem that's already been hidden.

46
00:02:59.679 --> 00:03:03.039
The comparison between detected and undetected conflicts

47
00:03:03.039 --> 00:03:05.600
comes down to what the reader is allowed to know.

48
00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:10.800
When a conflict is detected and annotated, the output tells the reader something like this.

49
00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.240
Source A reports that the policy was updated in 2022.

50
00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:18.639
Source B shows the policy was last revised in 2019.

51
00:03:18.639 --> 00:03:20.639
That difference hasn't been resolved.

52
00:03:20.639 --> 00:03:23.119
The reader now knows uncertainty exists.

53
00:03:23.119 --> 00:03:27.919
They can investigate, escalate, or accept the ambiguity with full knowledge.

54
00:03:27.919 --> 00:03:30.880
When a conflict goes undetected or unannounced,

55
00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:34.000
the reader sees a confident assertion with no indication

56
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:35.919
that another source said something different.

57
00:03:36.479 --> 00:03:40.399
One of those two reading experiences produces informed decisions.

58
00:03:40.399 --> 00:03:42.720
The other produces confident mistakes.

59
00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:47.119
Conflict annotation is the difference between an honest synthesis and a misleading one.

60
00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:52.639
Once a conflict is detected, your pipeline needs a rule for resolving it.

61
00:03:52.639 --> 00:03:54.320
There are three main strategies.

62
00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:59.039
PreferNewer says that when two sources conflict, the more recent one wins.

63
00:03:59.759 --> 00:04:02.960
This works well for domains where facts evolve over time.

64
00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:08.559
Regulatory requirements, software versions, and pricing data all fit this pattern.

65
00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:15.279
PreferAuthoritative says that designated high-trust sources outrank secondary ones.

66
00:04:16.239 --> 00:04:21.359
You need to define an authority hierarchy for your source corpus in advance.

67
00:04:21.359 --> 00:04:24.640
Authoritative sources might be official standards, bodies,

68
00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:27.279
regulatory agencies, or primary research.

69
00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:30.559
EscalateToHuman is the third option.

70
00:04:30.559 --> 00:04:36.239
When neither PreferNewer nor PreferAuthoritative can cleanly resolve a conflict,

71
00:04:36.239 --> 00:04:39.040
you route the decision to a human reviewer.

72
00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:42.640
This is expensive but appropriate when the stakes are high enough

73
00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:44.720
that an automated rule could get it wrong.

74
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:48.000
Here's a rule that's easy to overlook.

75
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:54.160
When you resolve a conflict, you should log it, even though the conflict is now closed.

76
00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:57.760
The resolution trail needs to be preserved for future reference.

77
00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:03.200
Your conflict log should record the conflicting claims and their source identifiers,

78
00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:06.079
the resolution strategy that was applied,

79
00:05:06.079 --> 00:05:10.239
and which claim was selected alongside the one that was discarded.

80
00:05:10.239 --> 00:05:12.720
Why does this matter after the fact?

81
00:05:12.799 --> 00:05:16.720
Because future reviewers need to know that a choice was made.

82
00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:21.839
If the conflict log doesn't exist, a reviewer reading the final output three months later

83
00:05:21.839 --> 00:05:25.519
will see a confident claim and assume there was consensus.

84
00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:29.760
With the log, they see that two sources disagreed,

85
00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:33.600
a rule was applied, and a specific choice was recorded.

86
00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:37.040
That's the difference between an auditable system and an opaque one.

87
00:05:38.239 --> 00:05:42.480
Conflict annotation is only useful if readers actually engage with it.

88
00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:46.959
That means the presentation has to be calibrated to the stakes and the audience.

89
00:05:46.959 --> 00:05:51.519
For high stakes claims, like legal conclusions or clinical findings,

90
00:05:51.519 --> 00:05:53.679
surface the conflict inline.

91
00:05:53.679 --> 00:05:56.160
Put it right next to the claim it affects.

92
00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:01.119
For medium risk conflicts, use a footnote or a collapsible detail block.

93
00:06:01.119 --> 00:06:05.440
The main body stays readable, but the detail is available one click away.

94
00:06:06.079 --> 00:06:10.640
For full document review, generate a conflict summary at the end.

95
00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:15.440
This gives senior reviewers a consolidated list of all detected disagreements

96
00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:18.480
without forcing them to read the entire document.

97
00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:21.679
The goal across all of these approaches is the same.

98
00:06:22.239 --> 00:06:27.040
Readers who need to know about a conflict should be able to find it without digging,

99
00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:30.079
and readers who don't need it shouldn't be buried in noise.

100
00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:35.519
Synthesis that hides disagreements makes a choice the reader can't see.

101
00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:39.679
Detection catches the conflict before the output is written.

102
00:06:39.679 --> 00:06:41.440
Annotation surfaces it.

103
00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:45.519
An explicit resolution strategy decides between the competing claims.

104
00:06:45.519 --> 00:06:48.399
And the conflict log preserves the trail,

105
00:06:48.399 --> 00:06:52.880
so a later reviewer knows a choice was made rather than assuming consensus.

106
00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:57.679
Surfaced conflict beats invisible certainty every single time.

107
00:06:58.399 --> 00:07:01.279
That wraps up this lesson. Let's move on.

